# coverage annotations (non-program rules)
{"rule": "DIAG-1", "covered_by": "compiler-diagnostics", "reason": "Every reject expectation names exactly one rule ID, and the runner requires an installed adapter to return that exact ID. Compiler tests additionally check structured rule and source locations for implemented frontend, resolver, and semantic failures. The full manifest claim becomes executable only when the adapter is installed."}
{"rule": "DIAG-2", "covered_by": "compiler-checked-program", "reason": "Semantic success in the candidate compiler publishes one private checked-program value, and lowering accepts that value rather than reparsing source or replaying a serialized artifact. Compiler tests inspect retained operations, cleanup edges, static obligations, and their dispositions for the implemented semantic families."}
{"rule": "DIAG-3", "covered_by": "compiler-trap-tests", "reason": "The candidate compiler retains one exact language-level trap record for each claim, and focused backend/host tests cover failed claims reaching that record before abort. The candidate specification defines no lifetime, implicit-check, or check-density report family."}
{"rule": "SCOPE-4", "covered_by": "compiler-claim-fault-injection-tests", "reason": "Focused backend tests first admit a true load-bearing claim, redirect only that checked claim condition to an existing false value in test-only typed IR, and observe abnormal termination with the exact CLM-1 record. No knowingly false source claim is admitted."}
{"rule": "EFF-4", "covered_by": "compiler-claim-fault-injection-tests", "reason": "The same typed-IR fault-injection tests inspect the emitted claim failure edge and execute it through wf_trap and abort; lowering places no language release or cleanup action on that trapping edge, so the no-unwinding/no-cleanup rule is tested without a false source claim."}
{"rule": "PROG-2", "covered_by": "source-envelope-tests", "reason": "Compiler source-bundle tests cover ordered logical records, exact path identity, duplicate and invalid paths, empty-unit rejection, and order-sensitive unit identity. These are invocation-envelope judgments rather than writer-emittable source-language verdicts."}
{"rule": "STOR-6", "covered_by": "compiler-target-layout-tests", "reason": "Selected-target representability and dynamic target-domain guards are non-source outcomes. Focused compiler tests cover complete static objects, runtime allocation arithmetic, element-address obligations, and the absence of source-rule or DIAG-3 attribution."}
{"rule": "CAP-1", "covered_by": "gated-family", "reason": "Capability-stub: Shareable/Sendable are reserved type-level capability predicates. v0 defines no thread construct and requires no kernel type to declare them, so the vocabulary is reserved but has no writer-emittable construct to satisfy or violate (SCOPE-1). Data-race impossibility is D1 law, not a per-program checkable constraint in the current surface."}
{"rule": "GATE-1", "covered_by": "gated-family", "reason": "Gated-family policy: editing any declared contract, signature, law bundle, storage contract, or gated-family member is one privileged, gated, audited toolchain operation outside steady-state writer capability. It is a process/policy rule with no writer-emittable construct (SCOPE-1), so no program case can express or violate it."}
{"rule": "LEDGER-1", "covered_by": "gated-family", "reason": "Gated-family policy: the single boundary-construct family (unsafe regions, FFI extern frames, trusted primitive imports) and its per-fact soundness-obligation ledger are AI-authored / human-approved through the gate; a kernel writer sees them only as opaque pre-approved library signatures. No writer-emittable construct (SCOPE-1), so it is covered by policy annotation rather than a program case."}
