{"id": "own5-neg-match-borrow-affine-payload-move", "rules": ["OWN-5", "OWN-13"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A shared match-derived affine payload binder aliases the borrowed enum and cannot be moved into an own destination."}
{"id": "own13-pos-uniq-match-payloads", "rules": ["OWN-13", "OWN-5", "OWN-7", "TYPE-7"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A local exclusive borrow match derives an &uniq payload projection that can be read through its binder while the overlapping parent holder remains frozen."}
{"id": "own1-pos-match-copy-payload-reuse", "rules": ["OWN-1", "OWN-13"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A user-enum match binder receives its concrete tag-only payload type, so repeated nested matches copy it instead of falsely consuming an untyped affine value."}
{"id": "own1-pos-propagate-copy-payload-reuse", "rules": ["OWN-1", "ERR-3"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A propagate binder records its concrete tag-only Ok payload type, allowing repeated Copy matches after the Result operand is consumed once."}
{"id": "form3-neg-region-param-missing-apostrophe", "rules": ["FORM-3"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A region parameter must use the apostrophe-prefixed REGIONID spelling; an IDENT in the region-parameter slot is rejected [FORM-3]."}
{"id": "own3-neg-undeclared-signature-region", "rules": ["OWN-3"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An uncalled function signature cannot cite an undeclared region; declaration checking rejects it under OWN-3."}
{"id": "type5-neg-wrong-region-arg-count", "rules": ["FN-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Explicit call-site region arguments must match the callee's declared region-parameter count. [TYPE-5] assigns the arguments a call site must state to the callee's class -- 'type, region, and const arguments for user generics [FN-2]' -- so a wrong region-argument count on a user function is FN-2's, and [DIAG-1] cites the rule the callee's class selects. The recorded rule was TYPE-5 until the 2026-08-08 ruling; that was wrong independently of any compiler change, and the id is kept because an id is a stable identifier rather than a claim."}
{"id":"op1-pos-tag-enum-equality","rules":["OP-1","OP-7","OP-8"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Tag-only enum equality is direct, nominal, and complement-correct for zero-, one-, and three-variant enums."}
{"id":"op1-pos-bool-enum-equality","rules":["OP-1","OP-7","OP-8","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Bool participates in the exact tag-only eeq/ene domain for all four ordered operand pairs."}
{"id":"op1-neg-eeq-distinct-enums","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"TYPE-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Equal-width tag-only enum types remain nominally distinct operands of eeq."}
{"id":"op1-neg-eeq-payload-enum","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A payload-carrying enum is outside the exact eeq domain. Its operands are moved, because a payload-carrying enum is affine and bare affine operands would earn the OWN-1 rejection first."}
{"id":"op1-neg-eeq-integer","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"eeq does not accept an integer type argument."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ene-integer","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ene does not accept an integer type argument."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ieq-tag-enum","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ieq remains integer-only and does not admit a user tag-only enum."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ieq-bool","rules":["OP-1","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ieq remains integer-only and does not admit Bool."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ine-tag-enum","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ine remains integer-only and does not admit a user tag-only enum."}
{"id":"op1-neg-ine-bool","rules":["OP-1","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"ine remains integer-only and does not admit Bool."}
{"id":"op1-neg-enum-ordering","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The enum equality family does not introduce enum ordering."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-eeq-implicit-type","rules":["FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A user-generic call stating no instantiation argument violates FN-2; instantiation arguments are always explicit. Repurposed 2026-08-08 by ruling: this case previously carried 'eeq requires its one explicit nominal enum type argument', a concern A1 deletes -- a table operation carries no written argument and 'eeq(left, right)' is legal v0.23 -- and [DIAG-1] gives a table operation the rule [OP-2] selects rather than FN-2 in any event. It was repurposed instead of retired because the missing-instantiation-argument half of FN-2's negative content would otherwise have had no live case: fn2-neg-implicit-instantiation, which carried it, is pending on a source A1 also made legal. The id is kept as a stable identifier rather than a claim."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-wrong-kind-instantiation-argument","rules":["FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An instantiation argument of the wrong kind: a const where the declaration binds a type. The count and the explicitness FN-2 requires are both satisfied, and the type-kind spelling compiles, so the kind is the only thing varied."}
{"id":"op1-neg-eeq-arity","rules":["OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"eeq has exactly two operands."}
{"id":"op1-neg-written-argument-on-deargumented-row","rules":["OP-1","TYPE-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A1 deletes the written type argument from every row outside the retained-argument class, so writing one is an invalid operation. The operands are the domain eeq accepts, so the written argument is the sole violation; with operands the row rejects, the domain violation cites OP-1 with the same kind and masks it."}
{"id":"fn8-pos-requires-eeq","rules":["FN-8","OP-1","OP-8"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Exact-domain enum equality is pure and total, may form the complete requirement goal, and is discharged by structurally identical caller evidence without adding equality to L0."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-requires-eeq-integer","rules":["FN-8","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OP-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A requires clause cannot widen eeq to the integer domain."}
{"id":"fn8-neg-requires-move-operand","rules":["FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"FN-8 names move in its own prohibition list. The operation, the domain and the clause shape are all legal here, and the same clause without move is accepted, so the moved operand is the sole violation."}
{"id":"x-nominal-bool-ops-run","rules":["OP-1","OP-8","PRE-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Every Boolean table operation executes through the ordinary compiler path, including true and false results."}
{"id":"x-nominal-multifield-payload-run","rules":["TYPE-2","GRAM-8","GRAM-10","OWN-13","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A mixed-width three-field enum payload is constructed, passed across a function boundary, destructured, and validated field by field."}
{"id":"set1-pos-local-and-field-copy","rules":["SET-1","TYPE-5","STOR-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"SET-1 updates a copy local and nested copy field through statement- and value-match joins; the selected values and untouched sibling are preserved."}
{"id":"stor1-neg-affine-set-target","rules":["STOR-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"SET-1 cannot replace a final affine Cell place; STOR-1 requires constructing a fresh owner under a new let."}
{"id":"own1-neg-set-rhs-kills-root","rules":["OWN-1","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The set target is valid before its RHS, but the RHS moves the target root; SET-1 post-state revalidation rejects the later commit under OWN-1."}
{"id":"const2-neg-set-target","rules":["CONST-2","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CONST-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A named constant is immutable static storage and cannot be selected as a set target."}
{"id":"x-array-const-checksum-run","rules":["CONST-1","CONST-2","TYPE-2","OP-1","OP-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A fixed immutable table feeds a loop-carried checksum through statically discharged bounds obligations and ordinary scalar-update paths."}
{"id":"x-array-mutable-checksum-run","rules":["TYPE-2","OP-1","OP-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A mutable fixed array is filled and folded through target-before-RHS checked indexed writes and ordinary checked reads."}
{"id":"x-buffer-mutable-checksum-run","rules":["TYPE-2","OP-1","OP-4","OP-9","SET-1","STOR-3","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A runtime-length primitive buffer is allocated, filled, folded, and normally freed through the compiler's checked heap-storage path."}
{"id":"x-struct-of-buffers-checksum-run","rules":["TYPE-2","OWN-1","SET-1","OP-4","OP-9","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A struct owns two parallel primitive buffers; projected roots support checked length, mutation, and reads, and normal owner exit releases fields in reverse declaration order."}
{"id":"x-buffer-borrowed-columns-run","rules":["OWN-3","OWN-4","OWN-5","OWN-7","OWN-10","OWN-12","TYPE-7","EFF-2","SET-1","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Distinct projected buffer fields are uniquely borrowed into a fill helper, then shared-borrowed into a fold helper; explicit dereference, provenance-based effects, checked indexing, and lexical loan expiry all execute through one compiler-independent program."}
{"id":"x-requires-output-capacity-run","rules":["FN-8","CLM-1","EFF-2","OWN-12","TYPE-7","OP-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Caller allocation equalities discharge the complete output-capacity goal before the call; the body-entry axiom then statically discharges the loop's indexed-read and indexed-write obligations while preserving the unique write effect, without a callee prologue."}
{"id":"x-crc32-standard-vector-run","rules":["OP-6","OP-1","OP-4","OP-9","SET-1","STOR-3","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"The standard CRC32 vector 123456789 computes 0xCBF43926 through checked byte-buffer access, the general total u8-to-u32 cvt path, and ordinary loop-carried integer operations."}
{"id":"x-option-byte-scanner-run","rules":["PRE-1","ERR-1","GRAM-8","GRAM-10","OWN-13","OWN-12","TYPE-7","EFF-2","OP-4","SET-1"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A shared-borrowed byte scanner returns Some(value: offset) for a present byte and None() for an absent byte through the ordinary concrete Option<u64>, function, match, and nominal lowering paths."}
{"id":"x-result-buffer-transform-run","rules":["PRE-1","ERR-1","GRAM-8","GRAM-10","OWN-1","OWN-13","OP-4","OP-9","SET-1","STOR-3","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A fallible fixed-size byte transform returns Result<buffer<u8>, DecodeError>; success transfers and matches the owned output, failure cleans temporary buffers, and unmatched Ok and Err owners use active-variant cleanup."}
{"id":"x-borrowed-pool-tree-run","rules":["TYPE-2","OWN-1","OWN-3","OWN-4","OWN-5","OWN-7","OWN-10","OWN-12","TYPE-7","EFF-2","SET-1","OP-1","OP-4","OP-9","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A structure-of-arrays pool builds and recursively validates a complete binary tree through whole-struct unique and shared borrows; projected buffer accesses, copy-field updates, caller-visible storage, effects, discharged obligations, explicit claims, and owner-only cleanup all use the normal compiler path."}
{"id":"x-wc-chunk-summary-run","rules":["TYPE-2","OWN-1","OWN-3","OWN-4","OWN-5","OWN-7","OWN-10","OWN-12","TYPE-7","EFF-2","SET-1","OP-1","OP-4","OP-9","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two owned byte chunks are summarized and associatively combined through unique output structs and shared input structs; whitespace state, early identity return, call-scoped loan overlap, caller-visible writes, discharged bounds obligations, and buffer cleanup execute through the normal compiler path."}
{"id":"x-base64-rfc-vectors-run","rules":["CONST-1","CONST-2","FN-8","CLM-1","OP-6","OP-1","OP-4","OP-9","OWN-1","OWN-3","OWN-4","OWN-5","OWN-10","OWN-12","TYPE-7","EFF-2","SET-1","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"The complete scalar base64 encoder executes RFC 4648 Man, M, and Ma vectors after each caller establishes the exact opaque output-capacity DAG, preserving the const alphabet, transferred inputs, unique output borrowing, exact integer widening, all three tail shapes, discharged bounds obligations, explicit claims, and owner cleanup."}
{"id":"x-child-reborrow-run","rules":["OWN-3","OWN-4","OWN-5","OWN-6","OWN-7","OWN-9","OWN-10","OWN-12","TYPE-7","EFF-2","SET-1","OP-4","OP-9","STOR-3"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Statement-scoped unique child reborrows of buffer and acyclic-struct holders pass through ordinary helper calls, project caller effects, suspend the parent only for the call statement, and resume it for later writes."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-missing-binding","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A conformance that omits the contract's second declared member is incomplete; FN-3 rejects at the conform closing brace and publishes no partial binding vector."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-extra-binding","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A conformance has one binding beyond its contract's complete member table; the extra binding is rejected by FN-3 rather than inferred as a member."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-out-of-order-binding","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A complete set of bindings written in a different order from the contract's member declarations is rejected by FN-3; signature matching does not reorder it."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-generic-contract","rules":["FN-2","FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The grammar admits a contract generics child, but the active specification defines no source contract template or monomorphization and rejects that child under FN-3."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-contract-arguments","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A source conformance must name one nongeneric source contract with no type arguments; the resolved contract argument list is rejected by FN-3."}
{"id":"fn3-neg-source-contract-bound","rules":["FN-2","FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Only the prelude Int and Float markers may bound a generic type parameter in the active specification; a successfully resolved source-contract bound is rejected by FN-3 and grants no behavior."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-normalized-region-effects","rules":["FN-1","FN-3","EFF-1","EFF-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A region-bearing member and function have positionally alpha-equivalent signatures; independently valid effect rows normalize to the same read-region set despite reversed payload order."}
{"id":"fn3-pos-empty-marker-conformance","rules":["FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A zero-member, zero-law marker contract accepts an empty conformance and needs neither a binding vector entry nor an FN-4 law record."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-returned-slice-inputs-run","rules":["FN-1","OWN-5","OWN-7","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"An own slice passes through one function and a same-region two-supplier function returns either direct input; call substitution preserves every permitted origin and the selected view reads the expected backing array."}
{"id":"fn1-pos-returned-slice-const-run","rules":["FN-1","OWN-5","OWN-10","CONST-2","OP-4"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A function returns a slice formed over immutable const storage in a caller-selected region; the signature ceiling includes immutable-const and the returned view reads the original bytes."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-returned-slice-arena-origin","rules":["FN-1","OWN-5","OWN-10","STOR-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An arena parameter can keep a formed view live for the written region, but it is not an exact direct input-slice supplier and its cleanup obligation cannot cross the return boundary."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-borrowed-slice-result","rules":["FN-1","OWN-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A top-level function whose rtype borrows a direct slice descriptor is rejected at that rtype because the signature carries no combined descriptor-place and viewed-data provenance summary."}
{"id":"fn1-neg-contract-borrowed-slice-result","rules":["FN-1","FN-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-1"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The same borrow-mode direct-slice rtype prohibition applies while forming a contract-member signature, before any conformance can bind an implementation."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-function-region-bearing-targ","rules":["FN-1","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A direct slice may be a function parameter or result, but supplying that region-bearing type as a user-function generic argument is an FN-2 source rejection."}
{"id":"fn2-neg-nominal-region-bearing-targ","rules":["TYPE-2","FN-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Even an empty source nominal cannot be instantiated with a region-bearing type argument; FN-2 rejects the targ independently of stored-field analysis."}
{"id":"stor5-neg-box-new-region-bearing","rules":["STOR-2","STOR-5","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"box_new accepts any ordinary operation type argument, but substituting a direct slice into box content is rejected by STOR-5 at the operation targ's complete type child."}
{"id":"stor5-neg-arena-new-region-bearing","rules":["STOR-2","STOR-5","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"STOR-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"arena_new cannot hide a direct slice in arena content; STOR-5 owns the region-bearing content rejection before unsupported arena execution or lowering matters."}
{"id":"own5-neg-slice-value-match","rules":["OWN-5","GIVE-1","FN-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"OWN-5"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A slice-valued let-initializer match is rejected at the complete value_match because v0.17 defines direct-return choice but no branch-dependent slice ownership join."}
{"id":"stor5-pos-direct-nested-slice-type","rules":["STOR-5","FN-2","OP-1"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"pending","reason":"The compiler intentionally leaves non-flat slice elements unsupported; v0.17 preserves this direct type-formation case rather than misreporting it as an FN-2 or STOR-5 source rejection.","doc":"A direct slice whose element type is another direct slice remains type-formable, and len may name that operation element type; no stored-content or nominal/function generic-argument position is introduced."}
{"id": "accept-sysentry-command-all-inputs", "rules": ["FN-7", "SYS-1", "SYS-2", "SYS-3", "SYS-10", "SYS-12", "SYS-13", "EFF-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A command entry selecting all four standard input rows in strictly increasing table-ordinal order is admitted [FN-7]; command.stdout and command.stderr are two distinct inputs sharing one type [SYS-12], and the entry's declared row is exactly the release contribution of the owned DirectoryRead's close attempt [SYS-10, EFF-2] because Args, Output, and the returned ExitStatus all release with no external effect."}
{"id": "accept-sysentry-command-no-inputs", "rules": ["FN-7", "SYS-3", "SYS-13"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A command entry that selects no standard input row is admitted and receives none [FN-7]. The complete SYS-3 declaration domain is present in every unit independently of the entry's selected inputs; this entry simply requests zero standard capabilities. exit_status is total and pure with no failure outcome [SYS-13]."}
{"id": "fn7-neg-missing-command-marker", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A main declaration with a named unit result but no `command` marker is not an alternate entry form. FN-7 rejects the missing marker, so every accepted program enters only through the uncallable command boundary."}
{"id": "reject-sys2-args-count-missing-region", "rules": ["SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SYS-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "All executable units admit the command system domain, so `args_count` resolves normally. The call supplies the correct borrowed Args value but omits its required region argument, leaving one SYS-2 signature fault."}
{"id": "reject-type5-system-arg-mismatch", "rules": ["TYPE-5"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A helper accepts the admitted opaque Args type, while its command caller passes unit. Exact argument conformance rejects the value-type mismatch under TYPE-5."}
{"id": "accept-sysname-near-lookalike", "rules": ["SYS-1", "SYS-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The system declaration domain reserves exact operation spellings, while an ordinary near-lookalike remains a source function and runs normally [SYS-1, SYS-3]."}
{"id": "reject-sysname-collision-in-kind-unit", "rules": ["SYS-1", "SYS-3", "TYPE-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-6"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The complete system declaration domain is present in every unit [SYS-3], so a source declaration equal to an inventory entry's spelling in the same domain is a collision, the unit is rejected, and neither declaration resolves [SYS-1]. No proximity, declaration order, scope depth, or expected type decides it. This is declaration-inventory rank 5, which cites TYPE-6 and reports only the system conflict."}
{"id": "reject-sysexit-return-u8-no-conversion", "rules": ["FN-7", "SYS-13", "FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ExitStatus is opaque with exactly one constructor and no implicit conversion from u8 [SYS-13, TYPE-4], so without the stated constructor a command entry's return value is unwritable. A return whose produced type differs from the enclosing function's written rtype is a hard error citing FN-1 at the return_stmt node, not a system-domain rule."}
{"id": "reject-form1-service-leading-construct", "rules": ["FORM-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The v0.33 entry grammar admits only the fixed `command` marker. A leading `service` spelling is an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1 before entry semantics."}
{"id": "reject-form1-embedded-leading-construct", "rules": ["FORM-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The v0.33 entry grammar admits only the fixed `command` marker. A leading `embedded` spelling is an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1 before entry semantics."}
{"id": "reject-form1-daemon-leading-construct", "rules": ["FORM-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FORM-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The v0.33 grammar no longer admits an arbitrary IDENT as a program kind. A leading `daemon` spelling is therefore an unknown construct rejected under FORM-1."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-label-out-of-order", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Selected standard inputs appear in strictly increasing table-ordinal order, so declared order is the one legal byte sequence [FORM-1, GRAM-8]. `command.cwd` (ordinal 1) written before `command.args` (ordinal 0) is out of order and is a hard error citing FN-7 at that input_label node."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-label-repeated", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Every standard input row is optional and each may be selected at most once; a repeated `command.args` label is a hard error citing FN-7 at that input_label node. Distinct binder identifiers do not make it two inputs, because ordinal identity, never binder or type identity, selects the supplied value."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-label-unknown", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The standard input table for kind `command` is closed at four rows, so `command.env` selects none and is a hard error citing FN-7 at that input_label node. A later kind table adds rows by specification amendment, never by a writer naming one."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-label-outside-entry", "rules": ["FN-7"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "No declaration other than the sole command entry carries an input_label child, so an input_label on a parameter of any other fn_decl is a hard error citing FN-7 at that input_label node. This keeps each standard input an explicitly selected command capability rather than an ambient authority any function can request."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-input-type-mismatch", "rules": ["FN-7", "TYPE-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A selected parameter's written mode and type equal its table row exactly, with no conversion, default, or inferred mode [TYPE-4, TYPE-5]. `command.args` written as `own DirectoryRead` is a hard error citing FN-7 at that complete param node, and the label rather than the written type is what selects the row."}
{"id": "reject-sysentry-call-to-kind-entry", "rules": ["FN-7", "PROG-3"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-7"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Main is the sole command entry and program start invokes it once [PROG-3]. It is never a source-callable function, so a source call resolving to main is rejected under FN-7."}
{"id": "accept-sysrelease-return-unit-declared", "rules": ["EFF-1", "EFF-2", "STOR-3", "SYS-4", "SYS-5", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The EFF-2 canonical case named by the specification: a nongeneric function whose only parameter is `own ReadFile` and whose complete body is exactly `return unit;` exhibits `external, blocks` and must declare exactly that row. The declaration contains no call, no claim, and no proof-required index, so its complete exhibited row is the release contribution of that parameter's compiler-derived close attempt on the return edge [STOR-3, SYS-5, SYS-11]. Release-complete means no terminal operation is needed to discharge ownership."}
{"id": "reject-syseff-return-unit-pure", "rules": ["EFF-1", "EFF-2", "STOR-3", "SYS-5", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "EFF-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The omission direction of the canonical case, and the one most likely to be missed because the function body has no syntactic effect at all. `pure` is the unique spelling of the empty row and excludes external and blocks exactly as it excludes every other category [EFF-1], so declaring it is an undeclared-but-exhibited rejection citing EFF-2 at that function's effects node."}
{"id": "reject-syseff-declared-unexhibited", "rules": ["EFF-2", "SYS-5", "SYS-9"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "EFF-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The opposite error for the two new categories, mirroring the existing eff2-neg-declared-unexhibited pattern: Args is an immutable value whose release is a logical consume with no host call and no external effect [SYS-5, SYS-9], and args_count's own row carries neither category, so declaring `external, blocks` is declared-but-unexhibited. Rows are checked both ways."}
{"id": "accept-syseff-conditional-release-union", "rules": ["EFF-2", "SYS-5", "SYS-6", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The release contribution is the union of every release action that may run on any edge of the conservative structural normal-control graph, so an owner released on only one match arm still contributes its row. Only the Ok arm ever holds a ReadFile; IoError takes no release row at all [SYS-5], and open_read's outcome type is the Result instantiation that makes the two arms the complete edge set [SYS-6]."}
{"id": "reject-syseff-conditional-release-narrow", "rules": ["EFF-2", "SYS-5", "SYS-6", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "EFF-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The near-miss a writer argues is correct by reading `conditional` as `optional`: running on only some paths never weakens the release contribution, and no path condition, constant evaluation, discharged law, optimizer fact, or backend reachability judgment removes an edge from that graph. Omitting the row is an undeclared-but-exhibited rejection citing EFF-2."}
{"id": "accept-syseff-pure-immutable-only", "rules": ["EFF-2", "EFF-3", "SYS-4", "SYS-5", "SYS-9"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The positive boundary of the same rule: an immutable value has no cursor, sequence position, or caller-visible mutation, and owning storage does not make it a resource [SYS-4]. args_count borrows Args and leaves it live [SYS-9], its release is a logical consume [SYS-5], and reading through a borrow of a current-function own root contributes no enclosing region effect, so `pure` is exact rather than merely permitted."}
{"id": "reject-syseff-pure-member-binds-release", "rules": ["FN-3", "EFF-2", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Kept separate from the EFF-2 omission cases because it exercises a different rule extension. A fn_sig has no body and no compiler-derived release, so it declares external and blocks without an EFF-2 judgment of its own; conformance equality then compares all six normalized capabilities by presence, and a `pure` member cannot bind a function that exhibits a category only through release. The mismatch is an FN-3 rejection."}
{"rule": "PATH-1", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "Relative-path construction is a runtime Result outcome, not a compile-time verdict: the dossier phrases that read like rejection (NUL, target-root prefix) are PathInvalid values the program matches. The catalog's group C cases state the behavior, and every one of them needs the fixture and argv arrangement this task's schema extension adds; they land with the runtime lane in task 0014. The no-allocation, no-copy, same-lease retype half is a codegen inspection with no source-to-verdict pair."}
{"rule": "PATH-2", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "Directory-relative resolution is process-equivalent by design and makes no confinement claim, so the observable content is a runtime open against a real directory fixture (group C/D), not an acceptance judgment. That a target with no directory-relative facility fails qualification rather than emulating the operation by prefix concatenation is a target-qualification property [QUAL-1] with nothing in a program to check."}
{"rule": "HOST-1", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "Code-unit width and family are properties of the selected target, not a portable source layout: no source construct observes them and no operation yields them as source values of a fixed width. The lossless-preservation guarantee is witnessed by the non-UTF-8 argv round-trip case, which needs the argv byte list this task adds and executes in task 0014; a target outside both families fails qualification instead."}
{"rule": "HOST-2", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "The two conversion routes differ only in what they validate and what they report, which is a runtime outcome comparison (Utf8Invalid, Utf8CopyInvalid, CopyTooSmall, and the sentinel-buffer no-write checks) rather than an acceptance judgment. The no-implicit-conversion half is TYPE-4's general rule, already exercised by the existing conversion-rejection cases and by reject-sysexit-return-u8-no-conversion."}
{"rule": "HOST-3", "covered_by": "compiler-checked-program", "reason": "The specification states outright that retention of lease identity in the checked program serves auditing and lowering, is not a source-acceptance judgment, and refuses no program. A lease is neither a borrow nor a region-bearing type, so STOR-5 places no restriction on storing one and OWN-5 provenance does not describe it: there is no source-to-verdict pair to write. The command-lifetime backing premise is a target guarantee enforced at qualification [QUAL-2]."}
{"rule": "SYS-7", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "The thirty portable classes are the failure vocabulary a running program branches on; the class-to-verdict content is the group D open-error cases, which need real filesystem fixtures. Exhaustive branching on the class is ERR-2's general judgment, already covered by the existing exhaustive-match cases, and a corpus case cannot observe a target's native-to-portable mapping or its use of Other and Unsupported."}
{"rule": "SYS-8", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "One-attempt transfer semantics are observable only by executing a transfer: a short success is not EOF, ReadBytes(next) is reported only for next > start, and the returned absolute endpoint becomes the next cursor. Invalid ranges are rejected statically before any host access and have no runtime trap fallback. These are the catalog's group E and F cases and require the file fixtures and redirection this task's schema adds. The signatures and call-scoped borrow modes themselves are SYS-2 inventory data."}
{"rule": "EFF-5", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "Sequential external-call ordering is a required property of every conforming lowering at facts-off and at every optimization level, not a source-acceptance judgment; no program is accepted or rejected by it. Its observable is call order in a combined sink, which is the catalog's stdout-and-stderr-to-one-sink case and needs the redirection description this task adds."}
{"rule": "QUAL-1", "covered_by": "compiler-target-qualification", "reason": "A target-qualification failure is explicitly not a source-language rejection and cites no language rule, so it can never be a reject verdict; this is the stop the new `unsupported` expectation kind exists for, and it becomes expressible once a case can pin an unqualified target. The qualification table is compiler-internal data and the language defines no registry, negotiation protocol, dynamic loading, or plugin interface, so no source construct reaches it."}
{"rule": "QUAL-2", "covered_by": "compiler-target-qualification", "reason": "All four stated guarantees are target properties with nothing in a program to prove: command-lifetime argument backing, a lossless code-unit family, directory-relative resolution, and directory enumeration. Qualification establishes each required guarantee before entry; absence stops compilation or startup rather than entering with a weaker substitute, returning a source outcome, or trapping."}
{"rule": "QUAL-3", "covered_by": "compiler-emitted-code-inspection", "reason": "The required emitted shape — static selection with no runtime operation-ID switch or handle lookup, at most one direct host call, no heap allocation, no data copy, no global lock, no per-call signal-disposition operation, and an inlined or immaterial wrapper — is established by inspection of emitted code and symbols, which the specification states is not a machine-checked language judgment. The one source-visible consequence, a closed output destination arriving as a recoverable outcome rather than a signal death, is the catalog's broken-pipe case."}
{"rule": "TRAP-1", "covered_by": "task-0014-runtime-lane", "reason": "A false executed claim attempts the mandatory DIAG-3 record and aborts the whole process without unwinding and without running language cleanup. Corpus claim-trap cases observe the record and termination, but nothing runs afterward to observe that no release, close, flush, or detach occurred, so the no-cleanup half is inspection-only. Process teardown reclaiming descriptors is a host property inside the SCOPE-3 trusted computing base."}
{"rule": "GATE-2", "covered_by": "gated-family", "reason": "A separation rule between the system domain and the boundary-construct family, in the same class as the GATE-1 and LEDGER-1 policy annotations. Its content is that a program calling system operations contains no gated construct and remains a kernel program, and conversely that general FFI and writer-declared external signatures stay reserved to that family. Neither direction has a writer-emittable construct to accept or violate: adding a system operation is a specification amendment, never a gate approval or a target-implementation act."}
{"id": "run-sysexit-code-0", "rules": ["SYS-13", "FN-7", "PROG-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-13: the target maps the returned command code exactly onto the host process status, and exit_status is total with no failure outcome. Zero is the first of the three codes the first slice's command programs distinguish; a conforming implementation supplies the declared standard inputs and invokes the entry once [PROG-3]."}
{"id": "run-sysexit-code-1", "rules": ["SYS-13", "FN-7", "PROG-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 1}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-13: the same mapping at a nonzero code. Kept as its own case because a target that collapsed every nonzero status onto one value would still pass the zero case, and the closed 0..255 range is the whole promise."}
{"id": "run-sysexit-code-2", "rules": ["SYS-13", "FN-7", "PROG-3"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 2}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-13: the third distinguished code, which together with the other two pins the mapping as the identity rather than a success or failure collapse."}
{"id": "run-sysarg-count-and-get", "rules": ["SYS-9", "SYS-6", "HOST-3", "QUAL-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "616c706861", "62657461"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-9: args_count is total and arg_get returns Ok at a present position and InvalidIndex beyond the last, returning no value. The arranged vector is the complete native one, so position 0 is the invoked name and the count is three; each returned HostString is an inline lease over the command-lifetime backing QUAL-2 requires, released by logical consume [SYS-5]."}
{"id": "run-syshost-nontext-argv-bytes-roundtrip", "rules": ["HOST-1", "HOST-2", "SYS-8", "SYS-9"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "61ff62"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Every argument retains its complete native code-unit sequence [HOST-1]. The start-zero lossless copy returns absolute endpoint 3, preserves the lone 0xff unchanged, and leaves the byte beyond `[start,next)` untouched [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-syshost-nontext-argv-utf8-invalid", "rules": ["HOST-2", "SYS-6", "SYS-9"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "61ff62"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "HOST-2: the text route on the same argument the lossless route copies verbatim. It validates the complete code-unit sequence and reports an explicit invalid-text outcome, never a replacement code point, a dropped code unit, or a truncated encoding. The two routes are the only way a host string reaches source content, and each names the route it takes."}
{"id": "run-syshost-copybytes-toosmall-unchanged", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-6", "HOST-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "616263646566"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-8: on every recoverable failure of a copy operation the whole buffer is unchanged, and CopyTooSmall(required) states the exact length the destination range must have for the same call to succeed. The program keeps running, so a sentinel prefill directly witnesses the no-write guarantee; invalid ranges are separate static rejections and never reach this outcome."}
{"id": "run-syshost-copyutf8-invalid-unchanged", "rules": ["SYS-8", "HOST-2", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "61ff62"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-8: host_copy_utf8 validates and measures the encoding first and returns Utf8CopyInvalid without writing any byte. The destination is large enough here, so only validation can have refused the transfer, and the surviving sentinel shows no partial encoding was written."}
{"id": "run-syshost-copyutf8-toosmall-unchanged", "rules": ["SYS-8", "HOST-2", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "616263646566"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-8: the other refusal of the text route, kept separate because one is a validation failure and the other a capacity failure. Valid text too long for the destination reports the exact encoded length through Utf8CopyTooSmall and writes nothing."}
{"id": "reject-syshost-copybytes-start-after-end", "rules": ["SYS-8", "ERR-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SYS-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Start greater than end refutes the first SYS-8 range goal statically. The rejected operation has no host call, source read, destination write, or runtime trap."}
{"id": "reject-syshost-copybytes-start-beyond-buffer", "rules": ["SYS-8", "ERR-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SYS-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An empty range is legal only when its endpoint lies inside the destination. Start equals end at 8 over a four-byte buffer, so the second SYS-8 goal is refuted statically."}
{"id": "reject-syshost-copybytes-end-beyond-buffer", "rules": ["SYS-8", "ERR-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SYS-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Start lies inside the destination but end exceeds its length. SYS-8 rejects the call statically instead of attaching an operation-internal trap."}
{"id": "run-syspath-relative-basic", "rules": ["PATH-1", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "666978747572652e747874"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "PATH-1: construction from one host string succeeds when the complete code-unit sequence contains no NUL code unit and begins with no target-root prefix. It consumes its input on success and on failure, and success retypes the same inline lease with no allocation and no code-unit change; the no-allocation half is inspection-only, and the Ok outcome is the testable half."}
{"id": "run-syspath-dotdot-preserved", "rules": ["PATH-1", "PATH-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "2e2f696e6e65722f2e2e2f696e6e65722f666978747572652e747874"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "PATH-1: the near-miss a writer expects to be rejected by analogy with confinement. Construction preserves every admitted code unit exactly, including . and .. components and every separator, and performs no normalization, canonicalization, prefix stripping, or component collapse. The first slice's directory capability is process-equivalent and makes no confinement claim [PATH-2]."}
{"id": "run-syspath-absolute-rejected", "rules": ["PATH-1", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "2f616273656e74"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "PATH-1: a Unix-family leading separator is a member of that target's target-root prefix set, so construction fails and yields no path value. This is a runtime PathInvalid outcome the program matches, not a compile-time rejection; PathError is an ordinary outcome type and never substitutes for IoError's InvalidPath [SYS-6]."}
{"id": "run-sysdir-open-notfound", "rules": ["SYS-7", "SYS-10", "PATH-2", "ERR-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "616273656e742e747874"]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-7: a target maps every native failure it can produce onto exactly one portable class, and the class is the sole portable discriminator that exhaustive control flow branches on [ERR-2]. Opening a name no object in the initial directory carries is NotFound. The case reads no detail field, because code and origin are diagnostic data and no portable semantics is defined in terms of them."}
{"id": "run-sysfile-empty", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-11", "SYS-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "656d7074792e747874"], "files": [{"path": "656d7074792e747874", "bytes": ""}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "On a zero-byte file the first read_once over a nonempty range is ReadEnd, never ReadBytes, because the latter carries a success endpoint strictly beyond start [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-sysfile-short", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "73686f72742e747874"], "files": [{"path": "73686f72742e747874", "bytes": "616263"}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A short success is not end of input. ReadBytes(next) names the absolute endpoint of the changed prefix `[start,next)`; only the following call that observes no byte reports ReadEnd [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-sysfile-exact", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "65786163742e747874"], "files": [{"path": "65786163742e747874", "bytes": "6162636465666768"}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A file exactly filling `[start,end)` returns ReadBytes(next: end), not ReadEnd. A further call is required before end of input can be reported [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-sysfile-multichunk", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-11"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "706167652e747874"], "files": [{"path": "706167652e747874", "bytes": "303132333435363738396162636465666768696a"}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Repeated reads advance the source cursor while each ReadBytes payload is the absolute endpoint of that call's changed prefix. The final short read changes only `[start,next)` and a later call reports ReadEnd [SYS-8]."}
{"id": "run-sysout-basic-write", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-12"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "One write_once makes at most one host output attempt. Its Ok payload is the absolute accepted endpoint, and the accepted source prefix is `[start,next)` [SYS-8, SYS-12]."}
{"id": "run-sysout-redirect-same-sink-order", "rules": ["EFF-5", "SYS-12", "SYS-8", "PATH-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"argv": ["63617365", "636f6d62696e6564"], "redirect": {"stdout": "combined", "stderr": "combined"}}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Sequential calls across two Output owners preserve source order [EFF-5]. Redirection makes them one sink; reading it back uses ReadBytes absolute endpoints to witness the combined bytes and ordering [SYS-8, SYS-12, PATH-2]."}
{"id": "form3-pos-index-ordinary-ident", "rules": ["FORM-3", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "v0.22 releases index from the fixed grammar atoms: an ordinary IDENT binding named index is declared and used as a subscript offset, pinning the released spelling [FORM-3, OP-4]."}
{"id": "sys2-neg-wrong-region-arg-count", "rules": ["SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SYS-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A system operation's call site states the region arguments its [SYS-2] row declares. [TYPE-5] assigns the written arguments by callee class -- 'region arguments for system operations [SYS-2]' -- and [DIAG-1] cites the rule that class selects, so stating two where args_count declares one is SYS-2's rejection rather than TYPE-5's. Added 2026-08-08: SYS-2 had no negative case and no representable citation before that date, so the coverage gate counted it covered on the strength of one accept row naming it."}
{"id": "ent3-pos-s11-counted-range-run", "rules": ["GRAM-4", "GRAM-6", "FN-1", "ENT-3", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A normal counted form executes 0..4 as an ascending half-open range, executes 4..4 zero times, and honors a break naming the counted loop after one local iteration [FN-1, GRAM-4, GRAM-6]. On both entered bodies, S11 proves the binder below the captured upper constant and thereby discharges each constant-array subscript without a claim or runtime bounds branch [ENT-3, OP-4]."}
{"id": "ent2-neg-counted-subscript-endpoint", "rules": ["ENT-2", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "ENT-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Each endpoint is a grammar-valid atom and each constant array subscript has its own discharged bounds obligation [OP-4], but ENT-2 admits only a preceding term or constant as a counted endpoint. A subscripted place remains excluded and is rejected at the first complete endpoint atom with the prescribed let-binding restructuring."}
{"id": "set1-neg-counted-binder-write", "rules": ["SET-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SET-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The counted binder is immutable compiler-updated own u64 state. A source set targeting it is rejected under SET-1 at the complete binder place; the hidden normal-fallthrough increment grants no source writability."}
{"id": "prv3-pos-external-branch", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-3", "ENT-3", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An args_count result is unconditionally external, but a real S1 branch establishes its bounds relation in both the ordinary and assertion-blinded states. PRV-3 therefore retains the accepted protected subscript."}
{"id": "prv3-neg-external-claim-conjunction", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-3", "ENT-3", "OP-4", "SYS-2", "CLM-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRV-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Assertion evidence laundered through signed Boolean decomposition is still assertion evidence: one claim over a band of two bounds establishes both members in the complete state, so complete-state OP-4 succeeds first, but the S3-blinded state removes the establishment and its decomposition together and PRV-3 rejects at the first `bytes[index]` psuffix, never at the preceding claim."}
{"id": "prv3-neg-external-claim", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-3", "CLM-1", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRV-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A named S3 claim is assertion evidence rather than proof for an unconditionally external constrained subject. Base discharge succeeds and the local protected leaf then rejects at the `bytes[index]` psuffix under PRV-3, not at the claim."}
{"id": "prv3-pos-internal-claim", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-3", "CLM-1", "CLM-2", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An uncontracted helper result derived from an internal literal remains internal. Its true, essential bounds residual may discharge the protected subscript without a provenance rejection [PRV-1, PRV-3, CLM-1, CLM-2, OP-4]."}
{"id": "prv3-pos-external-bound-only", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-3", "CLM-1", "CLM-2", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "External provenance controls the allocation length and bound, while the constrained offset is an internally reviewed helper result. Subject-only gating preserves the true essential residual [PRV-1, PRV-3, CLM-1, CLM-2, OP-4, SYS-2]."}
{"id": "prv2-pos-allocation-equality-call", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-2", "FN-8", "ENT-3", "OP-4", "OP-9"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "S5 literal equalities and S6 buffer allocation length prove the callee's exact requirement in the caller's unasserted state. The requirement-to-leaf bridge ends at that evidence and emits no PRV-2 event."}
{"id": "prv2-neg-nonexact-goal", "rules": ["PRV-2", "FN-8", "ENT-3", "OP-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A claimed non-strict index <= length goal does not prove the callee's exact strict index < length requirement. Full-state FN-8 rejects at the `read_strict` call node before any PRV-2 actual-atom target is formed."}
{"id": "prv2-neg-direct-system-result", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-2", "PRV-3", "CLM-1", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRV-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A helper's claim-backed protected leaf creates a direct parameter demand. Passing the unconditionally external args_count result produces exactly one PRV-2 event at the `index` argument atom of the entry's `claimed_read` call."}
{"id": "prv2-neg-entry-system-result-bridge", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-2", "FN-8", "ENT-3", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRV-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A command-entry claim proves the complete call goal but is absent from the caller's unasserted and S4-blinded states. The external system result cannot launder the callee leaf; PRV-2 selects the `index` argument atom of the entry's `required_read` call."}
{"id": "prv2-neg-two-hop-bridge", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-2", "FN-8", "ENT-6", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRV-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The exact same requirement protects calls through top and middle before reaching the leaf. The converged bridge composes both boundaries and produces one PRV-2 event at the `index` argument atom of the entry's `bridge_top` call."}
{"id": "prv2-neg-recursive-demand", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-2", "CLM-1", "FN-6", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRV-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A recursive function contains one claim-backed protected-leaf seed. The demand fixed point terminates despite its self-edge and selects the `index` argument atom of the entry's `recursive_read` call for the PRV-2 event."}
{"id": "prv2-neg-mutual-demand", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-2", "CLM-1", "FN-6", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRV-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "One member of a mutually recursive component owns the protected-leaf seed while the entry calls the other. Fixed-point composition selects the `index` argument atom of the entry's `mutual_right` call for the PRV-2 event."}
{"id": "prv2-pos-seedless-mutual", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-2", "FN-6", "FN-8", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A mutually recursive requirement cycle contains no protected leaf. A true helper-derived residual discharges the entry call goal, while the seedless provenance demand component remains empty [PRV-1, PRV-2, FN-6, FN-8, SYS-2]."}
{"id": "prv1-pos-payload-sibling-isolation", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-3", "CLM-1", "CLM-2", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Construction sends external data only to one enum payload projection. A dynamically reachable internal sibling carries a true bounds residual, proving that payload association alone adds no provenance edge [PRV-1, PRV-3, CLM-1, CLM-2, OP-4, SYS-2]."}
{"id": "prv3-neg-read-offset-taint", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-3", "CLM-1", "OP-4", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "PRV-3"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A real branch safely bounds the first `offsets[external_offset]` read, but that read joins its selector into the result. Reusing the result as a claim-backed offset rejects under PRV-3 at the later `bytes[derived_offset]` psuffix, not the first subscript."}
{"id": "prv1-pos-control-write-address-nontaint", "rules": ["PRV-1", "PRV-3", "CLM-1", "CLM-2", "OP-4", "SET-1", "SYS-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An external branch condition and write address add no provenance edge. The internal stored value and true bounds residual protect the later subscript without PRV-3 rejection [PRV-1, PRV-3, CLM-1, CLM-2, OP-4, SET-1, SYS-2]."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-plain-direct-result","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A verified plain-result postcondition is published at a fresh direct ordinary let, and the earlier callee summary discharges the relay's selected return before the runtime claim observes the value."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-ok-selected-receiver","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-5","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A verified Ok payload relation is transferred by a direct call match and its immediate bare payload-to-outer write; the caller's selected return and runtime claim observe the replaced value."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-no-selected-normal-exit","rules":["FN-9"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An Ok selector with only an Err normal return has no selected normal exit; FN-9 rejects at the selector with the residual no selected normal exit."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-unproved-selected-return","rules":["FN-9","ENT-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The first source-ordered selected return does not establish the declared output equality, so FN-9 rejects that unproved return even though the later return is discharged."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-entry-image-kill","rules":["FN-9","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An overlapping write permanently invalidates the formal's entry image before the selected return; FN-9 rejects the resulting unproved output relation."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-same-scc-summary","rules":["FN-9","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-9"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A seedless mutually recursive component cannot use a same-SCC postcondition summary to prove either returned call result; an independently proved recursive control in the same unit shows recursion alone is not the rejection."}
{"id":"ent3-pos-stage8b-bit-sources","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Unsigned direct-let iand establishes an independent result bound for each admitted operand, while direct wrapping shifts led by a typed literal one and an earlier named-constant one establish nonzero results; exact requirements consume all four S7 sources."}
{"id":"ent3-neg-stage8b-local-one","rules":["ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A local binding whose value equals one is not the direct typed-literal or earlier named-constant identity required by the shift source; the exact nonzero call requirement remains unproved and FN-8 rejects."}
{"id":"fn9-pos-direct-set-receiver","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","ENT-5","ENT-6"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A direct same-binding call result is established only after the target write; because the overwritten receiver formal is absent from the callee relation, the caller's selected return proves the replacement equality."}
{"id":"fn9-neg-named-outcome-no-publication","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Naming a whole Result before matching it carries no verified selected-payload publication token; the later exact equality requirement therefore remains unproved and FN-8 rejects the guard call."}
{"id":"ent5-pos-value-if-delivery-join","rules":["ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"Each value_if edge forwards its bare value relation, the continuation joins the unequal bounds at their weakest common bound, and the exact requirement plus both runtime branches observe the delivered value."}
{"id":"ent5-neg-value-match-no-delivery","rules":["ENT-5","FN-8"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A byte-similar value_match carries no forward-delivery relation from its arm-local claims, so the exact continuation requirement remains unproved and FN-8 rejects."}
{"id":"prv2-neg-complete-only-postcondition","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","PRV-1","PRV-2","FN-8","OP-4","SYS-2"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"PRV-2"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A body assertion proves the helper postcondition only in the complete view; using that summary to bridge an external system result into a protected subscript would launder the leaf, so PRV-2 rejects the exact call argument after FN-8 succeeds."}
{"id":"prv2-pos-postcondition-b-summary","rules":["FN-9","ENT-3","PRV-1","PRV-2","FN-8","OP-4","SYS-2"],"expect":{"kind":"accept"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unsigned direct iand proves the helper's bound in the S4-blinded view, so its verified B summary discharges the protected read without assertion or S4 laundering even when the source value is external."}
{"id":"clm3-pos-transitive-value-branch","rules":["CLM-3","FN-2","FN-6","FN-8","FN-9","GIVE-1","ENT-5"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"A deny_claims command root has one claim-free outgoing closure. A wfgrep-shaped candidate/prior value_if, a U-verified concrete generic relay, an internal required callee, and a seedless mutual SCC remain valid; the unrelated final drift test is a real branch, and no entry requirement wrapper exists."}
{"id":"clm3-neg-direct-unreachable-claim","rules":["CLM-1","CLM-2","CLM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CLM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A CLM-2-redundant claim with no ledger use still appears in a structurally unreachable arm. CLM-3 rejects at the complete claim statement; reachability, lifecycle disposition, and use count cannot erase a direct seed."}
{"id":"clm3-neg-generic-first-import","rules":["FN-2","CLM-1","CLM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CLM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A clean generic call precedes two claimed two-hop generic relay instances. CLM-3 rejects the complete claimed_relay<u64> call as the first source-ordered may-claim import, not the nested claim or later u8 call."}
{"id":"clm3-pos-upward-near-miss","rules":["FN-2","CLM-1","CLM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"run","exit":0},"status":"runnable","doc":"An ordinary claim-bearing caller invokes two concrete instances of one marked claim-free generic root. The strict closure does not flow upward, ordinary claim behavior is unchanged, and the program runs."}
{"id":"clm3-neg-mutual-scc-import","rules":["FN-6","CLM-1","CLM-3"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CLM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"A claim seed in one unmarked mutual-recursive component converges through the finite may-claim fixed point. CLM-3 rejects the marked root's complete call importing that component, even though this runtime input avoids the claim."}
{"id":"clm3-neg-static-conjunction-unproved","rules":["FN-8","CLM-3","ENT-4"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"FN-8"},"status":"runnable","doc":"Two true comparisons do not compose into an opaque `band` requirement in the closed entailment fragment. The internal call is rejected statically under FN-8; no command-entry wrapper or generated runtime check exists."}
{"id":"clm3-neg-body-check-bounds","rules":["OP-4","CLM-3","ENT-3","CLM-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CLM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The only complete-state proof of the array subscript is a claim in the marked root's own SCC. The claim event is judged before the strict U judgment, so CLM-3 rejects at the claim node and no OP-4 event at the [index] psuffix is fabricated; the literal caller actual does not specialize or seed the root body."}
{"id":"clm3-neg-body-check-requires","rules":["FN-8","CLM-3","ENT-3","CLM-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CLM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"The only complete-state proof of the required call is a claim in the marked root's own SCC. Claim import is tested before a strict FN-8 judgment at the same call, so CLM-3 rejects at the claim node and no FN-8 event at the required call is fabricated; the caller's literals are not copied into the root body. The callee retains its erased static requirement."}
{"id":"clm3-neg-transitive-check-summary","rules":["OP-4","CLM-3","ENT-3","CLM-1"],"expect":{"kind":"reject","rule":"CLM-3"},"status":"runnable","doc":"An unmarked callee's claim is its protected subscript's only complete-state proof. That claim enters the callee component's MayClaims set, so the marked root rejects at the importing call as one imported-claim event and no separate OP-4 event at the downstream leaf is fabricated."}
{"id": "set2-pos-affine-field-replace", "rules": ["SET-2", "TYPE-5"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SET-2: `let x = replace p = e;` exchanges the affine value at a writable place and binds the previous value as a fresh own binding. The target root stays live and is read again after the commit, and the moved-out value's sole owner is the new binding."}
{"id": "set2-neg-copy-target", "rules": ["SET-2", "OWN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SET-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SET-2 requires the target's final selected type to be affine under OWN-1. A copy-typed target is a hard error citing SET-2 at the complete target place, carrying the restructuring `use set for a copy place; read the previous value bare`."}
{"id": "set2-neg-region-bearing-target", "rules": ["SET-2", "STOR-5"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SET-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SET-2 requires the target's final selected type to be region-free under STOR-5. A slice<'r, U> binding carries a region, so replace on it is a hard error citing SET-2, carrying the restructuring that a slice's static origin set is fixed at initialization. Region-bearing types cannot occur in stored content, so this bites only a direct binding or dereference target."}
{"id": "set2-neg-rhs-type-mismatch", "rules": ["SET-2", "TYPE-5"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "After SET-2 derives a writable affine target place of type T, the right-hand side receives the same exact-`own T` judgment TYPE-5 states, located at the complete expr child of the replace_let_rhs. There is no mode coercion, type conversion, or target-selected overload, so a differently-typed replacement is a hard error citing TYPE-5."}
{"id": "op2-pos-overflow-obligation-discharged", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6", "EFF-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OP-2 constant-operand class: a bare + with a constant operand carries an overflow obligation rather than a runtime trap. A dominating branch establishes the residual, so the site is discharged: no runtime overflow check in any build mode, and no `traps` exhibited under EFF-2, which is why the row is pure."}
{"id": "op2-neg-overflow-obligation-undischarged", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A constant-operand-class call whose obligation the complete fact state does not discharge is a compile-time rejection citing OP-2 at its infix node, carrying the residual obligation rendered per ENT-6. The mechanical fixes are a dominating claim, a dominating branch, or the explicit wrap, checked, or sat respelling."}
{"id": "op2-pos-wrap-untouched-by-dissolution", "rules": ["OP-2", "OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The arithmetic-mode dissolution attaches obligations to the bare constant-operand class only. `.wrap` is untouched: total by definition, no obligation, no discharge needed, never traps, and no effect-row contribution, so an unbounded operand is fine and the row stays pure."}
{"id": "own6-pos-callresult-borrow-chain", "rules": ["OWN-6", "OWN-5", "OWN-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "OWN-6 reborrow extension: a callee determining one provenance-candidate parameter makes its borrow-mode result an ordinary borrow holder whose resolved place is the candidate actual's. The child fed to it in candidate position may take a caller-supplied region, composing a grandchild chain of depth two; creating that child suspends the parent for the remainder of its life, so no point sees two usable uniq paths to one place."}
{"id": "fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind-called", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The still-illegal form, now rejected at the boundary rather than at the binding: two uniq parameters in the result's formal region are two provenance candidates, so FN-1 rejects at the callee's complete rtype and OWN-6's binding-side rejection is unreachable. The caller here is retained deliberately, so the case pins that the declaration is still the reported error when a call site exists; fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind pins the same declaration with no caller at all."}
{"id": "const1-pos-forwarded-arithmetic", "rules": ["CONST-1", "FN-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-1 one-operation const arithmetic: a const-expression is at most one operation over two terms. `n * 2` is usable as an array<T, N> size and forwardable as a const targ, and each concrete instantiation evaluates it to one u64 value at monomorphization."}
{"id": "const1-neg-eval-overflow", "rules": ["CONST-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "CONST-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-1 const evaluation is exact in the unsigned 64-bit domain under the const-eval overflow policy const-reject: an operation whose mathematical result leaves that domain is a compile-time rejection citing CONST-1 at the complete const node. const-reject is disjoint from the runtime arithmetic modes and never traps."}
{"id": "const1-neg-runtime-arithmetic-mode", "rules": ["CONST-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "CONST-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-1: the const-expression tail reuses infix_op but its spelling must be one of the five bare operators. Const evaluation has no runtime overflow mode, so a mode-suffixed spelling is a hard error citing CONST-1 at the infix_op node; the grammar admits and the checker restricts."}
{"id": "const2-pos-struct-const", "rules": ["CONST-2", "TYPE-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-2 struct-typed consts: a source struct whose every field type is const-eligible is const-eligible. The cvalue takes the construction form naming its exact struct and writing every declared field in declared order, the const is laid out as one read-only static aggregate, and it is read via field suffixes exactly as subscript reads."}
{"id": "const2-neg-enum-not-const-eligible", "rules": ["CONST-2"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "CONST-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "CONST-2 const-eligibility is a closed list of primitives, arrays of const-eligible T, and source structs of const-eligible fields. Enums are not const-eligible and stay DEFERRED with recorded delta: a payload-enum const has no non-consuming read path, and a tag-only-enum const needs a constant-value family no current program demands."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-obligation-discharged", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6", "EFF-2"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An unsigned bare / is in the divisor class and carries the two-conjunct division obligation instead of a runtime test. A dominating branch discharges it in the complete state, the site keeps no zero-divisor check in any build mode, and the row is pure because the site exhibits no traps."}
{"id": "op2-neg-division-obligation-undischarged", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The same unsigned divisor-class site with nothing proving the divisor nonzero rejects at its infix node with residual `d != 0`. The dissolution replaces a runtime trap with a proof obligation; it does not substitute a trap for a missing proof."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-claim-backstop", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6", "CLM-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A dominating claim of the residual discharges a divisor-class site exactly as a branch does, but the claim itself carries the retained runtime check and the traps row, so the discharged division is free while the assertion is not."}
{"id": "op2-neg-division-constant-zero-divisor", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An integer-typed named const reads as an ENT-2 constant, so the site is in the divisor class, and its zero divisor is a ground false conjunct rejected at every non-contradictory point. There is no accepted always-trapping bare spelling."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-constant-divisor-total", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A nonzero constant divisor that is not -1 discharges both conjuncts with no fact source at all, so a signed n / 2_i32 is total and pure and emits one plain instruction."}
{"id": "op2-neg-division-minus-one-divisor-unbounded", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OP-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The one expressible signed-overflow shape: n / -1_i32 leaves the second conjunct as the residual `n != -2147483648` and rejects at the infix node."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-minus-one-divisor-bounded", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The same -1 divisor with a dominating branch bounding the dividend above i32::MIN discharges both conjuncts and keeps no runtime test."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-remainder-same-obligation", "rules": ["OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "% carries the identical obligation as / and discharges from the identical fact: one obligation family covers both spellings."}
{"id": "x-arith-idiv-trap-signed-two-variable-traps", "rules": ["OP-2", "CLM-1", "CLM-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A reviewed theorem about an uncontracted positive-divisor normalizer discharges the exact signed division domain and runs normally. The retained claim is also the typed-IR fault-injection base [OP-2, CLM-1, CLM-2, ENT-6]."}
{"id": "op2-pos-division-checked-untouched", "rules": ["OP-2", "OP-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The dissolution attaches obligations to the bare divisor class only. /checked is untouched: it needs no discharge, stays pure, and returns Err(DivideByZero()) for a zero divisor."}
{"id": "eff2-neg-division-class-site-pure-row", "rules": ["EFF-2", "OP-2", "CLM-1", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "EFF-2"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A claim that establishes a two-variable exact-division domain is now the function's sole trap contributor [CLM-1, OP-2, ENT-6]. Declaring that claim-bearing body pure is rejected under EFF-2; the subsequent discharged division itself contributes no trap effect."}
{"id": "eff2-pos-discharged-division-site-pure-row", "rules": ["EFF-2", "OP-2", "ENT-6"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The newly accepted class: a discharged divisor-class site exhibits no traps, so the narrower pure row is the correct one and a divisor-class function may declare a traps-free row for the first time."}
{"id": "fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Two same-kind same-region parameters are two provenance candidates, so FN-1 rejects at the complete rtype with no caller anywhere in the program. This is the case fn1-neg-result-provenance-two-same-kind-called cannot carry: it proves the declaration alone is the error."}
{"id": "fn1-neg-result-provenance-other-kind", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "FN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "Exactly one parameter is a candidate for the shared-borrow result, but another names the result's formal region in the other borrow kind. FN-1 rejects for that reason alone: candidacy is written-mode identity, not a count."}
{"id": "fn1-pos-result-provenance-distinct-regions", "rules": ["FN-1", "OWN-6", "SET-1"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "FN-1's own restructuring applied: the source parameter gets its own region, so exactly one parameter shares the result's region and kind. The caller binds the result holder, writes through it, and the write reaches the place the debtor parameter was borrowed from."}
{"id": "fn1-pos-result-provenance-zero-candidate", "rules": ["FN-1"], "expect": {"kind": "accept"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "The zero-candidate arm, on the contract-member surface where signature formation is the whole judgment. No parameter is a candidate, which is legal by elimination: OWN-10 admits no result-region borrow rooted in callee-local storage, so the only remaining source is named const storage."}
{"id": "sys14-list-outcome-exhaustive", "rules": ["SYS-6", "SYS-14", "ERR-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "612e747874", "bytes": "78"}, {"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "ListOutcome is closed over ListBytes carrying an absolute portable-prefix endpoint and entry count, ListEnd, and ListFailed. A match naming all three is exhaustive; this arrangement reaches ListBytes and exits 0."}
{"id": "reject-sys14-list-end-beyond-buffer", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-14", "ERR-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "SYS-8"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "list_once carries SYS-8's two static range goals. End 4104 exceeds the 4096-byte destination, so compilation rejects the call before any host enumeration or runtime trap."}
{"id": "sys14-list-zero-range", "rules": ["SYS-8", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "612e747874", "bytes": "78"}, {"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A zero-length destination range transfers nothing and yields ListBytes(next: start, entries: 0), never ListEnd. This case starts at zero, so the observed endpoint remains zero."}
{"id": "sys14-open-directory-component", "rules": ["SYS-14", "SYS-7", "PATH-2"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "open_directory validates its caller-owned name range before any host call. A range containing a target separator is not one path component, so it yields InvalidPath with both detail fields zero, no host call, and no capability."}
{"id": "sys14-open-directory-empty-name", "rules": ["SYS-14", "SYS-7"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An empty name range names no component and yields InvalidPath with both detail fields zero. The buffer holds a valid component and only the requested range is empty, which makes this the range judgment rather than a content judgment."}
{"id": "sys14-open-directory-success", "rules": ["SYS-14", "SYS-7"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "open_directory's success class: a name range holding one real component of the invocation directory yields a new DirectoryRead capability derived from the root capability, never from an ambient one. Exit 0 is reached only through Ok, so a build that answered every open with a validation failure would not pass."}
{"id": "sys14-list-handle-affine", "rules": ["OWN-1", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-1"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "A DirectoryList is an ordinary affine owner; nothing about a stateful system resource exempts it. Moving the handle out of its binding ends that binding's ownership, so a later unique borrow of the moved-from name is rejected before any traversal semantics is consulted."}
{"id": "sys14-list-handle-unique", "rules": ["OWN-5", "SYS-14", "SYS-4"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "OWN-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "SYS-14 declares no duplicate, split, rewind, or positioned-lane operation, so multiple lanes over one enumeration are not reachable. Two simultaneously live unique borrows of one handle are the one way source could try to build a second lane, and ordinary OWN-5 exclusivity rejects it: the family contract needs no rule of its own."}
{"id": "sys14-no-path-from-bytes", "rules": ["TYPE-5", "PATH-1", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "reject", "rule": "TYPE-5"}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "An entry name reaches source only as bytes: no operation turns an enumerated name into a HostString or a RelativePath, because a name's backing is not the command-lifetime argument snapshot while a path value is an inline lease over it. The gap is closed by the argument type at relative_path, not by convention."}
{"id": "sys14-directory-release", "rules": ["SYS-5", "SYS-14"], "expect": {"kind": "run", "exit": 0}, "arrange": {"files": [{"path": "612e747874", "bytes": "78"}, {"path": "737562", "directory": true}]}, "status": "runnable", "doc": "DirectoryList is release-complete: the value bound in the Ok arm leaves scope at the arm's end and compiler-derived release is the complete language obligation, so the program needs no terminal operation and this specification declares no explicit close. The corpus observes the program completing; the emitted module's single derived close is pinned by the compiler's own emission tests."}
